Ladybird reflects CSS animation keyframes from their declaration groups,
with computed values, per-frame easing and composite operations, and
underlying values for missing endpoints. It applies pending CSS changes
and computes values without publishing an effect stack or modifying its
animation overlays. CSS transitions expose their stored endpoint
values. Script-supplied keyframes retain their specified values and take
precedence over subsequent changes to matching CSS keyframe rules.

Blink’s KeyframeEffect::getKeyframes flushes pending animation updates.
CssKeyframeEffectModel::GetComputedKeyframes resolves CSS declarations
and supplies implicit endpoint values from the underlying style. Its
TransitionKeyframe::AddKeyframePropertiesToV8Object serializes stored
transition values. Ladybird follows this computed-value approach.

Gecko’s KeyframeEffect::GetKeyframes serializes declarations through
Servo_DeclarationBlock_SerializeOneValue, which uses the target’s style
to substitute variables but otherwise preserves specified CSS values.
CSSAnimationKeyframeEffect::GetComputedKeyframes fills missing endpoints
from base values. WebKit’s KeyframeEffect::getKeyframes distinguishes
parsed script frames from style-originated blending keyframes, fills
implicit endpoints, and also preserves specified text where possible.
Ladybird matches Blink when those engines retain relative CSS lengths.

Ladybird keeps explicitly declared endpoint frames before synthesized
frames at the same offset, matching Chrome and the imported WPT. It also
uses replacement endpoints for underlying values, including on additive
effects, and animation-wide easing for substitution-dependent easing.
The current CSS Animations draft instead prepends the initial frame and
uses the default composite and computed keyframe easing. The reflection
code marks these interoperability choices with AD-HOC comments.

The retained underlying style has already coordinated background lists
with background-image. Reflection preserves all explicitly declared
keyframe layers, but can’t recover discarded underlying layers. The
computed-value serializer also retains an explicit zero second argument
in translate(), and converts filter percentages to equivalent numbers.

Ladybird copies CSS-derived keyframe declarations into an independent
script effect, including custom properties, per-keyframe easing, the
resource context, and the pseudo-element target. Blink clones the
source model in KeyframeEffect::Create; StringKeyframeEffectModel::Clone
retains the declarations and default keyframe easing. Gecko copies
mKeyframes, mProperties, and mBaseValues in the KeyframeEffect copy
constructor. WebKit copies parsed and blending keyframes and the
pseudo-element identifier in KeyframeEffect::copyPropertiesFromSource.
The declaration-preserving reflection and playback behavior here follows
the Chrome reference tests.

Ladybird retains the zoom used to compute transition
endpoints and removes that zoom when reflecting or copying
those endpoints. Blink serializes its retained interpolation
values in TransitionKeyframe::AddKeyframePropertiesToV8Object
using a temporary style. Gecko removes effective zoom in
CSSPixelLength::to_animated_value and serializes retained
animation values from KeyframeEffect::GetKeyframes. WebKit reads
each retained blending-keyframe style through Style::Extractor in
KeyframeEffect::getKeyframes. These approaches preserve endpoint lengths
independently of the current target. Chrome omits property values with
a null target; Ladybird keeps its endpoint reflection without a target,
with the retained zoom applied.

Ladybird retains computed background-list entries beyond the image
layers for implicit CSS keyframes, while ordinary computed style
and inheritance keep coordinated lists. Blink resolves unanimated
property values in ResolveUnderlyingPropertyValues and includes them
in CssKeyframeEffectModel::GetComputedKeyframes. Gecko adds base-value
references in CSSAnimationKeyframeEffect::GetComputedKeyframes and
serializes them in KeyframeEffect::GetKeyframes. WebKit fills implicit
blending keyframes in KeyframeEffect::getKeyframes, preferring
matching rule declarations or underlying declarations before computed
serialization. The Chrome reference tests define the complete implicit
lists and the tail entries retained for active image layers in shorter
explicit keyframes. Relative lengths are computed for reflection, unlike
a path that returns authored declarations directly.
